---
import ProviderPlaybookTemplate from '../components/ProviderPlaybookTemplate.astro';
const data = {
 "kind": "manage",
 "name": "Cloudflare",
 "title": "How to Add and Manage DNS Records in Cloudflare",
 "description": "Add, edit, and delete DNS records in Cloudflare step by step, with the gotchas that break sites and mail, and free tools to verify every change.",
 "intro": "Managing DNS in Cloudflare is a short walk once you know where the records live and which defaults to watch. This is the complete flow, with the verification step most guides skip.",
 "panel": "The Cloudflare dashboard, dash.cloudflare.com: select your domain, then DNS, Records. Cloudflare is authoritative DNS for every domain on its nameservers, so all record management happens here, not at your registrar.",
 "steps": [
  [
   "Open the DNS panel",
   "dash.cloudflare.com, click the domain, then DNS, Records in the left menu."
  ],
  [
   "Add the record",
   "Click Add record. Pick the type (A, CNAME, MX...), enter the name (the @ symbol means the root domain) and the value. TTL is auto-managed by Cloudflare."
  ],
  [
   "Decide the proxy status",
   "The orange cloud (Proxied) routes traffic through Cloudflare's CDN and hides your origin IP; the grey cloud (DNS only) serves the raw record. Mail records (MX, SPF) must stay grey."
  ],
  [
   "Save and verify",
   "Changes are live on Cloudflare's edge in seconds. Verify with the DNS record lookup, then check worldwide agreement with the propagation checker."
  ]
 ],
 "quirk": "Cloudflare proxies (orange-clouds) most records by default. That's great for websites and terrible for mail: an MX or SPF-related record that must stay DNS-only will break email if proxied.",
  "fieldMap": { "name": "Name", "value": "Content", "ttl": "Auto (proxy-managed)", "apex": "@", "note": "The proxy status toggle (orange vs grey cloud) is Cloudflare-specific and decides whether the record is CDN-fronted or served raw." },
"faqs": [
  [
   "Does Cloudflare charge for DNS?",
   "No: the DNS plan is free for standard record counts, generous for any normal site."
  ],
  [
   "Can I proxy MX records?",
   "No, mail-related records must stay DNS only. Proxied mail is the most common Cloudflare misconfiguration."
  ],
  [
   "How long do DNS changes take in Cloudflare?",
   "The provider serves new values quickly, but resolvers keep old answers until each record's TTL expires. Verify live values with the record lookup, then watch the propagation checker until resolvers agree."
  ],
  [
   "Where do I find Cloudflare's nameservers?",
   "This playbook's nameservers guide covers the exact location. They are the delegation pair your registrar must point at for {name} to be authoritative."
  ],
  [
   "Should I lower TTL before changing records in Cloudflare?",
   "Yes, for anything planned: set TTL to 300 seconds at least one old-TTL cycle ahead, make the change, confirm propagation, then restore the TTL."
  ],
  [
   "Can I keep email with one provider and DNS in Cloudflare?",
   "Yes: DNS host and mail provider are independent. Publish the mail provider's MX, SPF, DKIM, and DMARC records in {name}, and keep those records unproxied."
  ],
  [
   "Which records should every domain have?",
   "A (and ideally AAAA) for the website, MX plus SPF/DKIM/DMARC for mail, and CAA to restrict certificate issuance. Everything else is per-service."
  ]
 ],
 "related": [
  [
   "DNS record types reference",
   "/dns-records/"
  ],
  [
   "TTL explained",
   "/what-is-ttl-in-dns/"
  ]
 ]
};
---
<ProviderPlaybookTemplate data={data} />
